   "is_root": true,
   "etymology": "From Proto-Albanian *ne uka (“not one”), from Proto-Indo-European *óynos (“one”) with negator *ne (“not”); typologically compare Latin nōn (“not”), noenum (“(Old Latin) idem”) (< ne + ūnus ~ ūnum). Cognates include Latin unicus, Gothic 𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌷𐌰 (ainaha), Old High German einac, Old Church Slavonic инокъ (inokŭ, “alone, only, single, sole”). Alternatively, Orel proposes *nuka, composed of *nu (< Proto-Indo-European *né, colored under the influence of the following labiovelar) and *ka (< Proto-Indo-European *kʷo- (*-kʷe), a pronomial stem); thus cognate to Latin neque (“and not”), Sanskrit नच (naca, “and not”), Gothic 𐌽𐌹𐌷 (nih, “and not”), Old Irish nach (“and not”).",
   "etymology_class": "inherited",
